WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.501
-I was wondering
if you ever see --

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.603
If you go on the Internet
or anything,

00:00:04.603 --> 00:00:08.474
you ever see a meme of yourself
as a Harry Potter meme,

00:00:08.474 --> 00:00:10.209
and you go like,
"That's pretty funny?"

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:12.378
-I have.

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:14.347
I think, like, maybe,
a couple over the years.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:17.083
but nothing that I can
immediately remember.

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:20.153
-I thought you --
-I don't seek them out.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:21.921
-I thought it would be funny
if I showed you a couple

00:00:21.921 --> 00:00:23.156
just so you could see.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:23.156 --> 00:00:25.991
-Yeah. Please.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:27.393
-They're funny.

00:00:27.393 --> 00:00:30.729
-Yeah, I feel like I should --
I should be educated.

00:00:30.729 --> 00:00:32.598
-Yeah. Here you go, yeah.

00:00:32.598 --> 00:00:34.734
Tell me you like them or not.

00:00:34.734 --> 00:00:36.269
This guy here is --

00:00:36.269 --> 00:00:38.204
-This is Harry Pugger.
I mean, it's --

00:00:38.204 --> 00:00:39.972
We're starting off,
you know, slow.

00:00:39.972 --> 00:00:41.141
-I mean that's --
What's not to like?

00:00:41.141 --> 00:00:42.375
That's great.
-That's great? It's cute.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:43.943
-Solid pun, cute dog,
-Yeah.

00:00:43.943 --> 00:00:48.614
-It's great.

00:00:48.614 --> 00:00:49.682
-If you're having
Quidditch problems,

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.084
I'll feel bad for you, son.

00:00:51.084 --> 00:00:54.287
I got 99 problems,
but a snitch ain't one.

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:57.090
[ Cheers and laughter ]

00:00:57.090 --> 00:00:59.124
That's a good one.
-That's pretty good.

00:00:59.124 --> 00:01:00.359
-That's what --

00:01:00.359 --> 00:01:03.696
You know, what's cool
about that stuff is that,

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:05.231
like, I never, as a kid,

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:07.066
and you're just insecure
at that age.

00:01:07.066 --> 00:01:10.469
I never felt cool at any point
playing that character.

00:01:10.469 --> 00:01:12.438
and sort of these things go
like, "No, that was --

00:01:12.438 --> 00:01:14.207
People thought that
was pretty cool.

00:01:14.207 --> 00:01:16.509
-Of course, it was cool.
Absolutely. Yes.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.644
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.647
Very cool.

00:01:20.647 --> 00:01:23.782
This one is one of my favorites.

00:01:23.782 --> 00:01:27.886
It says, "Ron, hold on!
Are those shoes on sale?"

00:01:27.886 --> 00:01:31.490
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:33.859
Ron, hold up.

00:01:33.859 --> 00:01:35.128
-That was --
That was what...

00:01:35.128 --> 00:01:36.495
-That was the actually
the dialogue.

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:37.563
-...I, the actor, was thinking.

00:01:37.563 --> 00:01:40.333
Yeah, absolutely.
-Actual dialogue.

00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:43.970
And this one here is...

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.571
"Thinking about
all them school loans,

00:01:45.571 --> 00:01:48.141
wondering if your wizardry
degree is gonna work out."

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:51.076
[ Laughter ]
This is a deep thought.

00:01:51.076 --> 00:01:52.511
-You know what that
actually is, though?

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:55.047
-Is that you in deep thought?
-No, that's me like --

00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:58.951
That's me, like, years ago,
out for lunch or something,

00:01:58.951 --> 00:02:01.287
realizing that there was
a paparazzi to my right

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.923
and being like, "Ugh.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:03.923 --> 00:02:06.325
Like, what do I do?"

00:02:06.325 --> 00:02:07.693
Yeah, that's
my deep thinking face.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:09.696
-My last one I want to show you
is actually a video.

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:12.531
and this was just everywhere
the other day.

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:14.133
It's called "Hermione Dancing."

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.935
I think this was at
a Comic-Con or something.

00:02:15.935 --> 00:02:18.137
But check this out.
I think this is funny.

00:02:18.137 --> 00:02:20.806
-♪ Here we go ♪

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:28.081
♪♪

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:29.248
[ Cheers and applause ]
-I mean, that's...

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:31.150
-I mean...
-Not bad.

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.052
-That is great.
-That's --

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:34.787
-I don't -- that --

00:02:34.787 --> 00:02:38.190
We need to remake the films
as a musical.

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:39.859
-We need more of that.
-Yeah.

00:02:39.859 --> 00:02:42.928
-Pal, I'm excited to see you
back on Broadway --

00:02:42.928 --> 00:02:45.031
"The Lifespan of a Fact."

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:46.732
-Yeah.
-This is a brand-new play.

00:02:46.732 --> 00:02:47.800
Why don't you tell us
about this?

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.702
-Yeah. So, as you can see,

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:52.371
it's myself, Bobby Cannavale,
and Cherry Jones.

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:53.772
We play --

00:02:53.772 --> 00:02:58.177
Well, I play a fact checker
that is sent to check an article

00:02:58.177 --> 00:03:00.246
written by an author,
played by Bobby.

00:03:00.246 --> 00:03:03.115
And this author is not used
to being held to kind of

00:03:03.115 --> 00:03:05.251
journalistic standards
of integrity --

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.652
of accuracy.

00:03:06.652 --> 00:03:07.953
So he's kind of --

00:03:07.953 --> 00:03:10.823
I just go in and make his life
hell for a few days.

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:13.526
And Cherry's character
is our editor,

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.162
who kind of has to,
like, make the decision

00:03:16.162 --> 00:03:18.330
whether or not to publish
the article in the end

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.433
and sort of keep us
from killing each other.

00:03:20.433 --> 00:03:21.734
But it's a comedy.
-It's comedy.

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:22.769
-I should say that as well.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.769 --> 00:03:24.103
-And the other thing I will say,

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.004
because it will get me
to see any play,

00:03:26.004 --> 00:03:27.907
is it's one act
straight through.

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.943
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Oh, I love that.

00:03:29.943 --> 00:03:31.711
-Yeah.
-Oh, I love that.

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:33.179
-Yeah, no. I'm...
Yeah.

00:03:33.179 --> 00:03:35.381
-But how are you gonna do this
and still be into football?

00:03:35.381 --> 00:03:37.549
'Cause Sunday is a big day
for Broadway.

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:41.087
-Yeah, me and Bobby have
been talking about this.

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:42.888
'Cause he's in
fantasy leagues, too.

00:03:42.888 --> 00:03:45.725
And we're just --
We got two shows on a Sunday.

00:03:45.725 --> 00:03:47.160
-Yeah.
-And, you know,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.061
that's when all the games are,
so it's just going to be --

00:03:49.061 --> 00:03:50.896
I have two exits in the play.

00:03:50.896 --> 00:03:51.997
Like, if you come
and see the play,

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:54.233
forget that I said this,
but I'll be --

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:57.536
There's basically, like,
a little cupboard that I go into

00:03:57.536 --> 00:03:58.838
at one point in the play.

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.773
I have, like, five minutes
in there.

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:03.842
I will have my phone
or something in there.

00:04:03.842 --> 00:04:05.778
-You'll be checking scores?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:06.946
I'll be coming out, saying --
-You'll be in the cupboard.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:08.114
You'll be like...

00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:09.982
"Awesome! Oh, my God."
-[ Chuckling ] Yeah.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.984
It will be like,
"Thank you, Jordan Howard."

00:04:11.984 --> 00:04:13.953
There will be stuff
like that going on.

00:04:13.953 --> 00:04:16.222
But no, it's --
Yeah, it's really --

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:17.256
I won't lie --
-Do you get in the thing --

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.558
Do you ever do a play

00:04:19.558 --> 00:04:21.227
and get distracted
or something like that

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:23.596
and you forget a cue
or a line or...

00:04:23.596 --> 00:04:26.732
-I'm pretty --
I'm generally --

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:30.102
Like, the fear of screwing up
keeps me concentrating.

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:32.438
But I've definitely had --
There are moments --

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.540
When you're in a bigger show
with a bigger cast, as well,

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:36.008
and there's, like,
stuff going on on-stage,

00:04:36.008 --> 00:04:41.313
you can kind of drift off and
look at the audience or see --

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:44.116
Well, 'cause you can't really
see beyond the third row.

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:46.519
But there will occasionally
be people up front

00:04:46.519 --> 00:04:47.787
that really grab your attention.

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.122
-Really?
-Yeah.

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:52.458
My favorite guy was a guy --
not, obviously, on this play --

00:04:52.458 --> 00:04:55.794
but I did "Rosencrantz and
Guildenstern are Dead"

00:04:55.794 --> 00:04:57.497
last year in London.

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:59.331
It's like --
It's a long play.

00:04:59.331 --> 00:05:00.700
It's, you know, sort --
-Yeah.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.268
-It's fun, but it's complex.

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:03.703
And this guy just, like,

00:05:03.703 --> 00:05:07.974
came into the first act,
sat down.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:10.710
My scene partner, Josh,
was doing most of the talking.

00:05:10.710 --> 00:05:13.445
so I was just able to watch
this guy come in,

00:05:13.445 --> 00:05:16.682
sit down with his rucksack
in the front row, put it down,

00:05:16.682 --> 00:05:18.918
rummage around in it
for a second,

00:05:18.918 --> 00:05:22.054
produce a foot-long
wrapped in tin foil...

00:05:22.054 --> 00:05:23.389
[ Laughter ]

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:27.059
...proceed to unwrap
half of it, go to town.

00:05:27.059 --> 00:05:30.930
So, like, the first two scenes,
just eating this massive thing.

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:32.664
-He's eating a giant hot dog.
-Eating a giant hot dog.

00:05:32.664 --> 00:05:34.667
And then, like,
as soon as he was done,

00:05:34.667 --> 00:05:36.468
put the stuff away
and went to sleep...

00:05:36.468 --> 00:05:39.672
[ Laughter and applause ]
...for the rest of the act.

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:42.274
-Totally worth the ticket price.
-Yeah, but that's the thing.

00:05:42.274 --> 00:05:44.043
He came back for the second act

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:45.344
and seemed to have a great time.

00:05:45.344 --> 00:05:47.046
He got up and stood at the end
and clapped.

00:05:47.046 --> 00:05:48.414
-Standing ovation?

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:50.048
He's like, "I'm coming back.
This is the best --

00:05:50.048 --> 00:05:52.050
-"Yeah, I got food."
-"Dinner."

00:05:52.050 --> 00:05:53.653
-Yeah, it was --
-Took a nap.

00:05:53.653 --> 00:05:55.153
-No, it was --
But that's the thing.

00:05:55.153 --> 00:05:56.855
Normally, you can like --

00:05:56.855 --> 00:05:58.891
You can end up getting
mildly irritated

00:05:58.891 --> 00:06:00.025
with guys like that
in the audience.

00:06:00.025 --> 00:06:02.061
but actually, that time,
we were just like --

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:03.362
-"I love this guy."
-"You're great."

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:04.830
[ Laughter ]
-I love this guy.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.999
-You don't care at all
that you're at a play.

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:08.434
-No, not all.

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:11.504
This one, you don't have time
to bring dinner and a nap.

00:06:11.504 --> 00:06:13.472
Congrats on this.
I will go see you in this one.

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:15.107
"The Lifespan of a Fact."

00:06:15.107 --> 00:06:17.543
Daniel Radcliffe, everybody.
[ Cheers and applause ]

